## Summary

`nf-gc` is a Nextflow plugin that provides lifecycle-aware garbage collection for work artifacts. It reclaims eligible intermediate outputs from a Nextflow work directory while the workflow is still running. The plugin tracks ownership, output provenance, downstream demand, publication, workflow-output retention, and deletion per concrete artifact path. It offers two garbage collection modes: `artifact` (default, fine-grained tracking) and `process` (coarser process-level boundaries).

## Get Started

Enable the plugin in `nextflow.config`:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;nf-gc@0.3.0&#x27;
}
```

No nf-gc-specific configuration is required. The default configuration is:

```groovy
nfGc {
    gc_mode = &#x27;artifact&#x27;
}
```

To use the coarser process clock explicitly:

```groovy
nfGc {
    gc_mode = &#x27;process&#x27;
}
```

Run a pipeline normally with the plugin configured, or select it from the command line:

```bash
nextflow run main.nf -plugins nf-gc@0.3.0
```

For lowest peak disk usage, prefer `publishDir mode: 'link'` when work and results paths are on the same filesystem, as hard-link publication does not duplicate the payload and gives nf-gc synchronous publication evidence.

To enable lifecycle tracing for testing and field runs:

```groovy
env.NF_GC_TEST_TRACE = &#x27;true&#x27;
```

## Examples

Basic plugin configuration with default artifact mode:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;nf-gc@0.3.0&#x27;
}

nfGc {
    gc_mode = &#x27;artifact&#x27;
}
```

Using process mode for coarser garbage collection:

```groovy
plugins {
    id &#x27;nf-gc@0.3.0&#x27;
}

nfGc {
    gc_mode = &#x27;process&#x27;
}
```

Publishing with link mode for optimal disk usage:

```groovy
process {
    publishDir = [path: { &quot;${params.outdir}/${task.process.replaceAll(/:/, &#x27;_&#x27;)}&quot; }, mode: &#x27;link&#x27;]
}
```

Running from the command line with the plugin:

```bash
nextflow run main.nf -plugins nf-gc@0.3.0
```

